Now one of the other advantages of CUDA, which makes it a very powerful tool for performance, is the underlining GPU architecture of it. Modern day GPUs contain thousands of small efficient cores that are designed specifically for highly parallel workloads and even also high bandwidth memory, which is very key because it needs to move extremely large amounts of data very quickly in comparison to a CPU. Another key concept is SIMT, or also Single Instruction Multiple Thread. This simply means that one single instruction is executed across multiple threads at the same time, which makes it perfect for these type of workloads such as deep learning, even image processing or even scientific simulations and any other workloads that have massive amounts of data.


SLIDE 4 — CUDA Programming Model 

CUDA_ Compute Unified Device Ar… 



CUDA actually uses a model that’s centered around a kernel. Now a kernel is just a regular function that runs on the GPU rather than the CPU. The overall flow of CUDA programming includes the following steps. First, the developer needs to write a kernel which simply describes what every thread needs to do. Second, the developer needs to allocate the GPU memory through the use of specific functions such as cudaMalloc. Third, the developer needs to transfer this data from CPU memory to GPU memory. Next, the kernel is launched using a grid and block configuration, which describes how many threads the kernel needs to run. And finally, after the GPU is done with the computing, the results need to be copied from GPU back to CPU and then the memory that has been used on the GPU also needs to be freed. So this model allows the developer to have a granular control over parallel executions.


SLIDE 5 — Thread Hierarchy 

CUDA_ Compute Unified Device Ar… 



CUDA works through a hierarchy of thread blocks and grids and a thread is the simplest execution unit. Threads are then organized into blocks which can be synchronized and share even also fast memory. Then, the blocks are organized into a grid which represents the entire kernel launch. So this fits into the GPUs hardware design, where each block is then scheduled onto Streaming Multiprocessors, and then the threads inside of the block execute in warps, where a warp is a group of 32 consecutive threads. So, understanding this type of hierarchy is actually very key because the way the threads are being grouped within threads blocks and then block grid, this actually effects memory access, even performance and also scalability.


SLIDE 6 — Memory Hierarchy in CUDA 

CUDA_ Compute Unified Device Ar… 



CUDA has a very detailed memory hierarchy. Now, on the top of this hierarchy, we have the global memory which is very large but also very slow to access. Below the global memory, we have shared memory which is very fast in comparison to the global memory and it is also shared by all the threads within a block and this is actually one of the most important tools for optimization. We then have each of the threads which have their own very fast registers. This is even also the fastest memory possible but the number of these available is actually limited. CUDA also has constant memory and even also texture memory, which both are cached, optimized for specific types of lookup patterns. So mastering this type of memory hierarchy is actually extremely key because this is key to writing high performance CUDA applications.


