So although CUDA can provide the developer with amazing performance levels, the key is to understand the optimization strategies that need to be applied in order to truly unlock the GPUs full power. So, for example, one of the very key optimizations which is also called memory coalescing. So memory coalescing means that the threads need to access memory in very predictable sequential patterns. Another strategy is called occupancy optimization, where a developer adjusts block sizes, register usages and even shared memory in order to maximize the number of threads that can be run on the GPU at the same time. And even also another key strategy is to actually reduce the warp divergence. Warp divergence happens when the threads inside of the same warp take multiple different paths of codes. Tools such as NVIDIA Nsight can help the developers to profile and optimize these very key kernels.

